    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Zafar Khan, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlaq-dynasty">Tughlaq dynasty</a>, made himself independent after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s invasion weakened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and declared himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-shah-i">Muzaffar Shah I</a> in 1407.</li><li>The sultanate's prosperity reached its height under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-begada">Mahmud Begada</a>, and its greatest extent under Bahadur Shah.</li><li>The Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> in 1573, and it became a Mughal subah.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-begada">Mahmud Begada</a>'s sobriquet Begada literally means the conqueror of two forts, probably after he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girnar">Girnar</a> (Junagadh) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champaner">Champaner</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavagadh">Pavagadh</a>).</p></aside>
    </section>

