    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Muzaffarid dynasty was founded by Zafar Khan, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-shah-i">Muzaffar Shah I</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-eaton">Richard M. Eaton</a> describes him as the son of a "peasant convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>". The 16th-century court historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-lodi">Sikandar</a> ibn Manjhu, in the Mir’āt-i-Sikandarī, says his family belonged to the Tank sect of Khatris; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> poem written for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-begada">Mahmud Begada</a> traces the line from the Solar dynasty, and some historians such as Aparna Kapadia consider the Muzaffarids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> status. His father Sahāran, titled Wajih-ul-Mulk, came from Thanesar.</p><p>In 1391 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Nasir-ud-Din <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq">Muhammad bin Tughluq</a> appointed Zafar Khan governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> with the title Muzaffar Khan, and in 1392 he defeated the former governor Farhat-ul-Mulk at the battle of Kamboi and occupied Anhilwada Patan. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s invasion devastated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, he declared independence in 1394. His son Tatar Khan imprisoned him in 1403 and ruled briefly as Muhammad Shah I before being poisoned. Freed, Zafar Khan declared himself Sultan Muzaffar Shah I in 1407, took the insignia of royalty and issued coins in his name.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sultanate's revenue came chiefly from the land. In the crown districts the state demand was nominally one-half of the produce, gathered through village headmen, called Patel or mukaddam in the north and Desai in the south. In the tributary states the chief's share rarely exceeded one-third of the crop and rarely fell below one-sixth, supplemented by special cesses and trade and transit dues. Towards the end of the reign of Muzaffar Shah II the ministers farmed the revenue on contract, and the Mirăt-i-Áhmedi reports large increases in what the land yielded, followed by confusion.</p><p>Its ports gave it a place in the sea trade, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-begada">Mahmud Begada</a> built a navy off the coast of Diu. The sultans built lavishly, above all in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmedabad">Ahmedabad</a>, founded by Ahmad Shah I on the Sabarmati River and styled Shahr-i-Mu'azzam, "the great city". Its monuments include the Jami Masjid of 1423, Teen Darwaza, Bhadra Fort and the Dada Harir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stepwell">Stepwell</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champaner">Champaner</a>, the 16th-century capital, survives as an archaeological park.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sultanate fought its neighbours for most of its history. Ahmad Shah I put down a rebellion of his uncles led by Firuz Khan, repelled Hushang Shah of the Malwa Sultanate, and led expeditions into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> in 1419, 1420, 1422 and 1438; he defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> and annexed Thana and Mahim. Muhammad Shah II forced the ruler of Idar to submit and took tribute from Dungarpur, but was driven back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champaner">Champaner</a> in 1449. Ahmad Shah II defeated the Malwa sultan at the Battle of Kapadvanj, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-begada">Mahmud Begada</a> took the forts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girnar">Girnar</a> and Champaner.</p><p>In 1519 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-sanga">Rana Sanga</a> of Chittor defeated a joint army of Malwa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, later invaded Gujarat and plundered its treasuries; after his death the sultans recovered and sacked Chittor fort in 1535. The Portuguese took Diu in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-diu-1509">Battle of Diu</a> in 1509. The sultanate was, after the Bahmani Sultanate, the second state in the subcontinent to use firearms and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> artillery extensively. Under Muzaffar Shah III its army drew free soldiers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and enslaved men forced to fight from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, and many of its specialist warriors were Habshi, Africans.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> was divided into the khálsah, the crown domain ruled directly, and tributary lands left under their former rulers in return for tribute in service or money. The tribute depended on the terms each chief had accepted, and was sometimes collected by military expeditions led by the king, called mulkgíri. Inside the tributary states justice and revenue were managed as under the Chaulukya kings.</p><p>The crown territory was divided into districts, sarkars, either assigned to nobles to support troops or kept as crown domains under paid officers called muktiă, who kept the peace and collected revenue with soldiers sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmedabad">Ahmedabad</a> and fortified posts, thánás, under thánadárs. Each sarkar was split into parganáhs under an ámil or tahsildár. Ahmad Shah I ruled that when a district governor was a royal slave his accountant must be free, and the reverse, so each would check the other. The sultanate comprised twenty-five sarkars. Succession was often settled by the nobles, who raised and deposed sultans, and under Ahmad Shah III they divided the kingdom among themselves.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sultanate created a distinctive Indo-Islamic architecture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, which took elements of the earlier Maru-Gurjara style into mihrabs, roofs, doors, minarets and facades. Its 15th-century buildings are known for paired, slender, richly carved minarets, drawing on the skills of local stone-carvers who had worked on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples; the "shaking minarets" of the Jama Mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmedabad">Ahmedabad</a> fell in an earthquake in 1819. Mosques such as the Jama Masjid at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champaner">Champaner</a>, the Sidi Bashir Mosque and the Sidi Sayyed Mosque, and the complex at Sarkhej Roza, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-begada">Mahmud Begada</a> enlarged as a summer retreat, belong to this style, which anticipates Mughal mihrabs, minarets, jali screens and chattris.</p><p>The sultanate also produced a large Persian historical literature, from the Tarikh-i-Muzaffar Shahi to the Mirat-i-Sikandari, written soon after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s conquest, and the Arabic Zafarul-Walih bi Muzaffar wa Alih by Hajji Dabir. Its wars shaped the balance of power among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khandesh">Khandesh</a>, the Bahmanis and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> states.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sultanate came under attack from both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> and the Portuguese. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> in 1535 and briefly occupied it, during which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>, Bassein and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daman">Daman</a> passed to the Portuguese. Bahadur Shah regained his kingdom but was killed by the Portuguese on board a ship while making a deal with them in 1537. He left no son; after a disputed succession the nobles chose Mahmud Shah III, who struggled with nobles seeking independence and was killed by his servant in 1554. Under Ahmad Shah III the nobles divided the kingdom, and he was assassinated in 1561.</p><p>In 1573 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> annexed Gujarat and it became a Mughal subah. The last ruler, Muzaffar Shah III, was taken prisoner to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>. He escaped in 1583 and with the nobles' help briefly regained the throne before Akbar's general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-rahim-khan-i-khanan">Abdul Rahim Khan-i-Khanan</a> defeated him in January 1584. He took refuge with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jam-sataji">Jam Sataji</a> of Nawanagar State, whose forces fought the Mughals at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bhuchar-mori">Battle of Bhuchar Mori</a> in 1591. He finally killed himself when he was handed over to the Mughals.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Gujarat Sultanate, founded by Zafar Khan as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-shah-i">Muzaffar Shah I</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s invasion weakened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, ruled western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmedabad">Ahmedabad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champaner">Champaner</a>. It prospered under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-begada">Mahmud Begada</a> and reached its widest extent under Bahadur Shah, left a rich Indo-Islamic architecture, and fell to Portuguese pressure and Mughal conquest, ending with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s annexation in 1573.</em></p>

